// Card content refreshed 2026-09-15 after a full UpToDate clinical review of all medication
// and condition cards (dosing, titration, monitoring, thresholds, contraindications).
// Educational reference only — verify against institutional protocols, pharmacy guidance,
// smart-pump libraries, and active orders.
const meds=[
["Norepinephrine","Vasopressor","First-line catecholamine vasopressor for distributive shock; predominantly alpha-1 vasoconstriction with some beta-1 activity. Use after assessing volume responsiveness and the cause of shock.\n\nDosing: Label example: start 8–12 mcg/min and titrate to perfusion/BP; average maintenance 2–4 mcg/min. Weight-based ranges are common but protocol-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: Premixes include 16, 32, or 64 mcg/mL. Confirm concentration, central-versus-peripheral policy, pump guardrails, and a dedicated compatible lumen.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to the ordered MAP and perfusion target. Trend continuous BP, rhythm, capillary refill, mentation, urine output, lactate, extremity temperature, and infusion site. Reassess after every rate change. Avoid abrupt discontinuation—taper gradually while expanding volume. For weight-based dosing in obesity, use ideal body weight (UpToDate additions).","Warning flags: Extravasation can cause tissue necrosis; stop through the affected line and follow the local antidote pathway. Watch for arrhythmia, excessive afterload, and digital/mesenteric ischemia. A rising dose is a prompt to reassess source control, volume status, cardiac function, acidemia, and occult obstruction—not just raise the pump.\n\nSource: FDA norepinephrine prescribing information"],
["Vasopressin","Vasopressor","Non-catecholamine V1-mediated vasoconstrictor used as an adjunct when vasodilatory shock remains hypotensive despite fluids and catecholamines. It may reduce catecholamine exposure but can worsen cardiac output or ischemia.\n\nDosing: Fixed 0.03 units/min added to norepinephrine is the common approach (not titrated); usual range 0.01–0.04 units/min. Doses above 0.04 units/min are reserved for salvage therapy because of ischemic risk (UpToDate correction—the old 0.07 upper bound is removed). Dose and weaning are protocol-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: Concentrate may be diluted in NS or D5W to 0.1 or 1 unit/mL; ready-to-use premixes also exist. Verify the dispensed product before programming the pump.","Titration & monitoring: Follow the ordered fixed-dose or titration strategy. Trend MAP, perfusion, urine output, sodium, rhythm, cardiac output when available, and skin/digital/abdominal ischemic signs.","Warning flags: Escalate for bradycardia, falling cardiac output, hyponatremia, or coronary, mesenteric, skin, or digital ischemia. Vasopressin is a vesicant—ensure proper catheter placement. When discontinuing, taper by 0.01 units/min every 30–60 minutes and do not stop vasopressin before norepinephrine (clinically significant hypotension reported). Consider adding it when norepinephrine requirements are escalating (e.g., 0.2–0.5 mcg/kg/min). Vasopressin is not a substitute for source control or adequate resuscitation; clarify whether the unit tapers it or stops it after catecholamine needs improve (UpToDate corrections/additions).\n\nSource: DailyMed vasopressin label"],
["Epinephrine","Vasopressor / inotrope","Potent alpha- and beta-adrenergic agonist that increases vascular tone, contractility, and heart rate. Used for refractory shock and in indication-specific anaphylaxis and cardiac-arrest pathways.\n\nDosing: Septic-shock range: initial 0.01–0.2 mcg/kg/min IV, usual 0.01–0.5 mcg/kg/min (non–weight-based 1–40 mcg/min), titrated to the ordered MAP (UpToDate correction—the old 2 mcg/kg/min ceiling exceeded the stated usual range). Anaphylaxis and ACLS doses/routes are different—use the correct emergency algorithm.\n\nDilution/preparation: Ready-to-use premixes include 16 or 64 mcg/mL. Concentrated 1 mg/mL product requires indication-specific dilution; verify formulation carefully to prevent concentration errors.","Titration & monitoring: Use continuous BP and ECG. Trend perfusion, urine output, glucose, potassium, lactate, chest pain, and infusion site; titrate to the ordered hemodynamic endpoint and wean gradually when stable.","Warning flags: Watch for severe hypertension, tachyarrhythmia, myocardial ischemia, pulmonary edema, hyperglycemia, hypokalemia, and extravasation injury. Epinephrine can raise lactate through beta-2 stimulation, so interpret the trend with perfusion and the whole clinical picture. Per UpToDate: use invasive arterial BP monitoring during continuous infusion; there are no absolute contraindications to injectable epinephrine; extravasation can be managed with phentolamine or terbutaline infiltration.\n\nSource: DailyMed epinephrine label"],
["Phenylephrine","Vasopressor","Predominantly alpha-1 agonist that raises SVR with little direct beta activity. Consider when vasodilation is prominent and added chronotropy is undesirable, while recognizing that increased afterload may lower stroke volume.\n\nDosing: Common ICU infusion range 40–180 mcg/min IV, titrated to effect; initial IV bolus 40–100 mcg for acute hypotension, repeatable every 1–2 minutes (UpToDate correction—the old 100–200 mcg bolus exceeded the initial bolus dose). Local maximums vary — follow the active order set.\n\nDilution/preparation: The 10 mg/mL vial must be diluted. Use the pharmacy-standard concentration and confirm the pump library before starting.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to the ordered MAP/BP goal. Trend continuous BP, HR/rhythm, peripheral perfusion, urine output, cardiac output when available, and the infusion site.","Warning flags: Stop or reduce per order for severe bradycardia, falling cardiac output, hypertension, ischemia, or extravasation. If BP rises while perfusion worsens, reassess stroke volume and the cause of hypotension rather than treating the number alone. Per UpToDate: not recommended for septic shock except when norepinephrine causes serious arrhythmias, cardiac output is known high with persistent hypotension, or as salvage therapy; acidosis reduces its efficacy—correct acidosis and ensure adequate volume before escalating.\n\nSource: DailyMed phenylephrine label"],
["Dobutamine","Inotrope","Primarily beta-1 inotrope used for low-output states when contractility support is needed and BP can tolerate beta-2 vasodilation. It may improve forward flow without reliably increasing MAP.\n\nDosing: Initial 2–5 mcg/kg/min (0.5 mcg/kg/min noted only for less severe decompensation); usual range 2–10 mcg/kg/min; maximum 20 (UpToDate correction—the old \"start 0.5–1, usual 2–20\" conflated usual with max). Higher rates are uncommon and protocol-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: Premixes and pharmacy concentrations vary; 1,000 mcg/mL is common. Do not mix with sodium bicarbonate or strongly alkaline solutions (confirmed in the DailyMed dobutamine label). For weight-based dosing in obesity use ideal body weight; dobutamine may be a vesicant—ensure proper catheter placement (UpToDate additions).","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to the ordered cardiac-output/perfusion endpoint. Trend continuous ECG, HR, BP, urine output, lactate/perfusion, ectopy, and cardiac output/filling pressures when available.","Warning flags: Tachyarrhythmia, ectopy, ischemia, or hypotension may limit therapy. Correct hypovolemia before escalation when appropriate; a faster heart rate without better perfusion is not a successful response.\n\nSource: DailyMed dobutamine label"],
["Amiodarone","Antiarrhythmic","IV antiarrhythmic used for recurrent ventricular arrhythmias and selected atrial tachyarrhythmias under an indication-specific order.\n\nDosing: For recurrent VF or unstable VT: 150 mg in 100 mL D5W over 10 minutes, then 1 mg/min for 6 hours, followed by 0.5 mg/min. AF with RVR requires an indication-specific strategy; do not automatically apply the ventricular-arrhythmia regimen.\n\nDilution/preparation: The loading dose is commonly prepared as 150 mg in 100 mL D5W. Dilute in D5W only—incompatible with normal saline (precipitation) per UpToDate. Verify the maintenance concentration, line requirements, in-line filter, compatibility, and pump library against the dispensed product and institutional policy.","Titration & monitoring: Use continuous ECG and BP monitoring. Trend rhythm, ventricular rate, QTc, potassium, magnesium, liver function, and the infusion site. Reassess after the loading dose and with any clinical or rate change.","Warning flags: IV amiodarone can cause rate-related hypotension, bradycardia, AV block, QT prolongation, and infusion-site injury. Slow or stop and escalate per order/protocol for these findings. Correct low potassium or magnesium and review other QT-prolonging medications. Per UpToDate, the half-life is measured in days to weeks (up to 40–55 days with chronic therapy)—adverse effects may persist long after discontinuation.\n\nSource: DailyMed amiodarone IV label"],
["Diltiazem","Rate control","Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker that slows AV-nodal conduction for selected atrial fibrillation/flutter or SVT patients. Confirm the rhythm and hemodynamic stability before use.\n\nDosing: Initial bolus 0.25 mg/kg IV over 2 minutes; if needed, 0.35 mg/kg after 15 minutes. Infusion commonly starts at 5–10 mg/hr and may increase to 15 mg/hr for up to 24 hours.\n\nDilution/preparation: Use the premix or reconstitute exactly per product instructions; confirm concentration and pump limits.","Titration & monitoring: Use continuous ECG and BP. Titrate to the ordered ventricular-rate goal; trend rhythm, HR, BP, symptoms, perfusion, and signs of worsening heart failure.","Warning flags: Avoid or escalate with hypotension, cardiogenic shock, severe HFrEF/decompensated systolic failure, significant AV block without pacing, sick-sinus syndrome, or AF with pre-excitation. Treat the patient and rhythm—not an isolated heart-rate number. Per UpToDate: avoid diltiazem in patients taking a beta-blocker; an additional bolus can be given when increasing the infusion rate for more immediate onset (a lower 5–15 mg bolus if hypotension is a concern).\n\nSource: DailyMed diltiazem label"],
["Propofol","Sedative","Rapid-onset, short-acting hypnotic for intubated, mechanically ventilated adults. It provides sedation but no analgesia; pair pain treatment with an ordered sedation target. Per UpToDate: propofol lacks analgesic properties—assess pain, not just RASS. Egg allergy is a labeled contraindication, but the AAAAI statement failed to demonstrate increased risk in egg/soy-allergic patients—do not withhold automatically without allergy input.\n\nDosing: Label guidance starts at 5 mcg/kg/min, increasing by 5–10 mcg/kg/min no faster than every 5 minutes. Typical ICU maintenance is 5–50 mcg/kg/min; higher exposure increases risk and is protocol-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: Standard emulsion is 10 mg/mL and is generally used undiluted. Use strict aseptic technique, dedicated tubing per policy, and observe product-specific hang-time/discard limits.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to RASS and ventilator synchrony. Continuously monitor BP and respiratory status; trend triglycerides, total lipid calories, CK/lactate/acid-base status when clinically indicated, and daily sedation goals.","Warning flags: Hypotension, apnea, hypertriglyceridemia, pancreatitis, and infection risk are key concerns. Suspect propofol infusion syndrome with unexplained metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, bradyarrhythmia, cardiac failure, or renal injury—stop and escalate urgently per protocol. Remember the lipid calories.\n\nSource: DailyMed propofol label"],
["Dexmedetomidine","Sedative","Alpha-2 agonist that supports lighter, arousable sedation with relatively little respiratory depression. Useful around spontaneous-breathing trials or extubation when clinically appropriate, but it is not reliable deep sedation.\n\nDosing: Label maintenance: 0.2–0.7 mcg/kg/hr (UpToDate's usual range is wider, 0.2–1.5 mcg/kg/hr—no added benefit above 1.5). A 1 mcg/kg load over 10 minutes is labeled, but ICU protocols often omit it because of bradycardia/hypotension; higher ranges are institution-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: Common ready-to-use concentration is 4 mcg/mL. Concentrate requires dilution; administer through a controlled infusion device.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to ordered RASS and clinical goal. Continuously monitor HR, BP, oxygenation, mental status, and comfort; consider hepatic dose reduction and monitor for withdrawal after prolonged exposure.","Warning flags: Bradycardia, sinus pauses, hypotension, and transient hypertension with loading can occur. Escalate for symptomatic bradycardia or hemodynamic compromise. A calm, arousable patient may still need separate analgesia. Per UpToDate: dexmedetomidine is dosed in mcg/kg/HOUR—unit misinterpretation errors have been reported, so verify pump programming. The manufacturer recommends ≤24 hours (studies support longer); withdrawal (hypertension, tachycardia, delirium, agitation) is more likely after prolonged use.\n\nSource: DailyMed dexmedetomidine label"],
["Fentanyl","Analgesic","Potent, rapid-onset opioid for acute pain and analgesia-first sedation in mechanically ventilated patients. It can improve ventilator tolerance but does not replace a separate sedation assessment.\n\nDosing: Common ICU examples are 25–50 mcg IV boluses or a 0.5–3 mcg/kg/hr infusion, titrated to effect; these ranges are protocol-dependent and should be reduced for frailty or interacting sedatives.\n\nDilution/preparation: Commercial injection is commonly 50 mcg/mL; pharmacy infusion concentrations vary. Confirm mcg—not mg—units, concentration, compatibility, and controlled-substance handling.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to the ordered pain score/CPOT and ventilator goal. Monitor RR, SpO2, ventilation/ETCO2 when available, BP, HR, sedation, bowel function, urinary retention, and cumulative exposure.","Warning flags: Respiratory depression, apnea, hypotension, bradycardia, ileus, chest-wall rigidity after rapid/high-dose IV administration, and interaction with other CNS depressants require vigilance. With prolonged infusion, plan for accumulation, tolerance, and withdrawal; reassess pain before escalating sedation. Per UpToDate: half-life prolongs with infusion duration due to lipid accumulation—expect prolonged sedation/respiratory depression after stopping long infusions; serotonin-syndrome risk with concomitant serotonergic agents; use adjusted body weight if weight-based dosing in obesity (non–weight-based dosing preferred).\n\nSource: DailyMed fentanyl label; ICU analgesia dosing study"],
["Midazolam","Sedative","Short-acting benzodiazepine used for selected procedures, seizures, and ICU sedation when clinically indicated. It provides anxiolysis, amnesia, and anticonvulsant effect but no analgesia.\n\nDosing: If a load is needed, 0.01–0.05 mg/kg IV slowly; continuous infusion 1–8 mg/hr non–weight-based is preferred (UpToDate correction—the old weight-based 0.02–0.1 mg/kg/hr differs from this approach; if weight-based, use adjusted body weight). Use smaller doses in older, debilitated, or hemodynamically unstable patients and with opioids.\n\nDilution/preparation: For continuous infusion, the label describes dilution of 5 mg/mL product to 0.5 mg/mL in NS or D5W. Verify the pharmacy concentration and pump library.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to ordered RASS and indication. Monitor BP, respiratory rate, SpO2/ventilation, mental status, and cumulative dose; reassess daily for awakening and continued need when appropriate.","Warning flags: Respiratory depression, apnea, hypotension, paradoxical agitation, and prolonged awakening can occur. Accumulation is greater with prolonged infusion, obesity, renal/hepatic dysfunction, or interacting opioids. Benzodiazepine exposure can worsen delirium; use the lowest effective dose. Per UpToDate: consider a daily awakening trial or nurse-protocolized sedation; the active metabolite accumulates in renal dysfunction, causing prolonged awakening.\n\nSource: DailyMed midazolam label"],
["Ketamine","Analgesic / sedative","Dissociative anesthetic used for induction/RSI, procedural sedation, and adjunct analgesia or sedation.\n\nDosing: RSI: 1–2 mg/kg IV. Analgesia: 0.1–0.3 mg/kg IV slowly or 0.1–0.4 mg/kg/hr infusion. Continuous ICU-sedation dosing is protocol-dependent. Do not give the 100 mg/mL concentration as an IV bolus without proper dilution. Some experts reduce the RSI induction dose by about half in hemodynamic instability; in Class 3 obesity use adjusted or ideal body weight per protocol (UpToDate correction/addition).\n\nDilution/preparation: Verify the vial concentration and use the pharmacy- or institution-standard infusion concentration. Label syringes clearly; concentrated product may require dilution before IV administration.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to the ordered pain and RASS targets. Reassess BP, HR, respiratory pattern, SpO2, ETCO2 when available, mental status, airway patency, and secretions after boluses and rate changes.","Warning flags: Ketamine may increase secretions and cause emergence reactions. Stop or escalate for apnea, respiratory depression, severe hypertension or tachyarrhythmia, laryngospasm, or an unsafe airway. Maintain immediate access to suction, oxygen, and ventilation support.\n\nSource: DailyMed ketamine label; multi-society consensus"],
["Heparin","Anticoagulant","Unfractionated heparin used for therapeutic anticoagulation or prophylaxis according to the indication and active institutional protocol.\n\nDosing: Common VTE example: 80 units/kg IV bolus, then 18 units/kg/hr. Lower-intensity starts, omitted boluses, dose caps, and dosing weight are indication-specific; follow the active order set.\n\nDilution/preparation: A common infusion concentration is 25,000 units in 250 mL (100 units/mL). Confirm the dispensed concentration, pump library entry, and independent double-check before starting or changing the infusion.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate only with the active anti-Xa or aPTT nomogram. Trend anti-Xa/aPTT, CBC, hemoglobin/hematocrit, platelets, bleeding, and new thrombosis. Recheck levels at the interval specified after initiation and every rate change. Per UpToDate, the therapeutic anti-Xa target is commonly 0.3–0.7 units/mL; patients over 60 may have higher levels/longer aPTTs. Many heparin concentrations exist (1–20,000 units/mL)—verify the dispensed concentration independently.","Warning flags: Stop or notify per protocol for active bleeding, a critical coagulation result, suspected HIT, new thrombosis, or a major platelet fall. Consider HIT when the platelet trend and timing fit the clinical context; do not adjust from a generic nomogram when an indication-specific protocol is ordered.\n\nSource: UW Medicine protocols; practical heparin guidance"],
["Insulin","Metabolic","Regular insulin infusion is used for DKA/HHS, critical-care hyperglycemia, or—by a separate bolus protocol—to shift potassium intracellularly. The indication determines the dose, target, fluids, and monitoring.\n\nDosing: Severe DKA example: 0.1 units/kg/hr IV after fluids and potassium assessment; reduce and add dextrose as glucose falls while continuing until ketoacidosis resolves. Hyperkalemia commonly uses 5–10 units regular insulin IV with dextrose. Non-DKA glycemic-control dosing is protocol-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: A common infusion is 100 units in 100 mL NS (1 unit/mL). Use the institution’s priming, tubing, independent-check, and pump procedures.","Titration & monitoring: Follow the active protocol exactly. Check glucose at the specified frequency—often hourly during titration—and trend potassium, anion gap/beta-hydroxybutyrate, bicarbonate/pH, osmolality, and neurologic status as indicated. Coordinate dextrose and potassium replacement.","Warning flags: Hypoglycemia and hypokalemia can be rapid or delayed. Hold/escalate per protocol for significant hypokalemia; do not stop DKA insulin solely because glucose normalizes if ketoacidosis persists. After insulin for hyperkalemia, continue glucose checks for delayed hypoglycemia. Per UpToDate: after the insulin/dextrose bolus for hyperkalemia, infuse D10 at 50–75 mL/hr (a dextrose bolus alone is not their approach); expect potassium to fall 0.5–1.2 mEq/L. Keep glucose 150–200 mg/dL until DKA resolution criteria are met, and continue dextrose-containing fluids alongside the insulin infusion as glucose falls.\n\nSource: 2024 hyperglycemic-crises consensus report; Queensland adult electrolyte guideline"],
["Sodium bicarbonate","Buffer","Alkalinizing agent reserved for selected severe metabolic acidosis, hyperkalemia with metabolic acidosis, sodium-channel–blocker toxicity, and other specific protocols. It does not correct the underlying cause of acidosis and generates CO2.\n\nDosing: Reserve IV bicarbonate for documented severe metabolic acidosis or indication-specific toxicologic protocols (poison-control, nephrology, or critical-care guidance). For hyperkalemia: bicarbonate has limited acute efficacy even with metabolic acidosis and should not be the only treatment—when given acutely, UpToDate uses an isotonic solution (150 mEq in 1 L D5W over 2–4 hours); hypertonic ampule boluses (50 mEq/50 mL) are potentially hazardous (UpToDate correction—the old \"50 mEq IV over 5–15 minutes\" example is removed). Multiple doses cause hypernatremia.\n\nDilution/preparation: 8.4% solution contains 1 mEq/mL; 4.2% contains 0.5 mEq/mL. Verify concentration, line compatibility, sodium/volume load, and whether dilution is required.","Titration & monitoring: Reassess pH/PaCO2, bicarbonate, sodium, potassium, ionized calcium, volume status, ECG, and ventilation after each dose. In toxicity, trend the ordered ECG and pH endpoints rather than giving repeated empiric doses.","Warning flags: Hypernatremia, alkalemia, hypokalemia, reduced ionized calcium, volume overload, tissue injury with extravasation, and worsening intracellular acidosis if ventilation cannot clear CO2 are key risks. Calcium must not be given in bicarbonate-containing solutions (calcium carbonate precipitation). Never mix in the same line with incompatible calcium or catecholamine solutions; flush per policy (UpToDate additions).\n\nSource: DailyMed sodium bicarbonate label; Queensland adult electrolyte guideline"],
["Rocuronium / succinylcholine","RSI paralysis","Neuromuscular blockers used to facilitate RSI. Neither provides sedation, amnesia, or analgesia; induction must precede paralysis, followed immediately by post-intubation analgesia and sedation.\n\nDosing: Rocuronium RSI: 1.5 mg/kg IV using total body weight—the dose explicitly preferred by UpToDate's RSI topic (correction—the old 0.6–1.2 mg/kg cap is removed). Succinylcholine adult label average: 0.6 mg/kg IV; many RSI protocols use 1–1.5 mg/kg. Follow the active airway order set.\n\nDilution/preparation: Rocuronium is commonly supplied 10 mg/mL and succinylcholine 20 mg/mL. RSI boluses are usually undiluted; verify the exact product, concentration, weight scalar, and syringe label.","Titration & monitoring: A single RSI dose is not titrated. Confirm induction, airway/ventilation readiness, and post-intubation medication plan before administration. If ongoing blockade is ordered, use train-of-four and the local neuromuscular-blockade pathway.","Warning flags: Maintain immediate ability to oxygenate and ventilate. Avoid succinylcholine after the acute phase of major burns, crush injury, denervation, upper motor-neuron injury, or myopathy because of potentially fatal hyperkalemia; assess malignant-hyperthermia, bradycardia, and prolonged-apnea risk. Rocuronium may cause anaphylaxis or prolonged paralysis—have sugammadex immediately available when using rocuronium for RSI (rapid reversal if intubation fails); remember rocuronium paralyzes ~45 min versus succinylcholine 6–10 min. A 20 mL saline bolus immediately after the rocuronium bolus may speed onset. Paralysis can hide pain and seizures—sedation and analgesia remain mandatory (UpToDate additions).\n\nSource: DailyMed rocuronium label; DailyMed succinylcholine label"],
["Nicardipine","Antihypertensive infusion","Titrated arterial vasodilator for short-term control of severe hypertension when oral therapy is not feasible. Choose the BP target from the acute organ-injury syndrome; the same number is not safe for every diagnosis.\n\nDosing: Start 5 mg/hr. Increase by 2.5 mg/hr every 5 minutes for rapid titration or every 15 minutes for gradual titration; maximum 15 mg/hr.\n\nDilution/preparation: Ready-to-use premixes include 20 mg/200 mL (0.1 mg/mL) and 40 mg/200 mL (0.2 mg/mL). Match the concentration to the smart-pump entry.","Titration & monitoring: Continuously monitor BP and HR; titrate to the ordered SBP/MAP goal and reassess neurologic, cardiac, renal, and peripheral perfusion. Use a large peripheral or central vein and inspect the site frequently per policy.","Warning flags: Avoid precipitous BP reduction. Stop or reduce for unacceptable hypotension or tachycardia; watch for headache, phlebitis/site injury, worsening angina, or heart failure. Contraindicated in advanced aortic stenosis. Once the target is reached, reduce to the lowest effective rate. Per UpToDate: oral→IV conversion equivalents are roughly 20 mg q8h oral ≈ 0.5 mg/hr IV, 30 mg q8h ≈ 1.2 mg/hr, 40 mg q8h ≈ 2.2 mg/hr; do not stop abruptly in coronary disease—rebound angina can occur.\n\nSource: DailyMed Cardene IV label"],
["Nitroglycerin","Vasodilator infusion","Venodilator with dose-dependent arterial effects used for acute coronary ischemia, cardiogenic pulmonary edema, and selected hypertensive emergencies when BP and preload permit. Response is clinical—not simply a pump rate.\n\nDosing: Start 5 mcg/min. Increase by 5 mcg/min every 3–5 minutes initially; if response is absent at 20 mcg/min, larger 10–20 mcg/min steps may be ordered. Follow the diagnosis-specific goal.\n\nDilution/preparation: Premixes include 100, 200, and 400 mcg/mL; 50 mg/250 mL equals 200 mcg/mL. Use non-adsorptive tubing and the product-specific filter policy.","Titration & monitoring: Continuously monitor BP and HR. Titrate to ordered BP plus relief of chest pain, work of breathing, or pulmonary edema; reassess perfusion and hemodynamics after each rate change.","Warning flags: Stop or reduce for hypotension, syncope, worsening ischemia, or marked bradycardia/tachycardia. Avoid with recent PDE-5 inhibitor exposure or riociguat and in preload-dependent states such as RV infarction or tamponade. Headache is common; tolerance can develop. Per UpToDate: avoid in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with LV outflow-tract obstruction, constrictive pericarditis, and increased intracranial pressure; nitroglycerin may decrease heparin's anticoagulant effect—monitor aPTT/anti-Xa when co-infused.\n\nSource: DailyMed nitroglycerin IV label"],
["Furosemide infusion","Loop diuretic infusion","Continuous loop diuretic for acute pulmonary edema or severe volume overload when controlled diuresis is desired. Response depends on renal function, prior loop exposure, perfusion, and the sodium/volume delivered elsewhere.\n\nDosing: Protocol-dependent. A common adult approach is an IV loading dose followed by 5–20 mg/hr, individualized to renal function, prior dose, and response. UpToDate allows undiluted direct IV at 20–40 mg/min (correction—the old \"do not exceed 4 mg/min\" was far more restrictive than UpToDate); rapid/high-dose IV exposure still increases ototoxicity risk, so keep continuous infusions at the lowest effective rate.\n\nDilution/preparation: A common pharmacy concentration is 100 mg/100 mL (1 mg/mL). Confirm concentration, compatibility, access, and pump settings.","Titration & monitoring: Titrate to the ordered urine-output, net-balance, weight, symptom, or filling-pressure goal. Track strict I/O, hourly urine output, daily weight, BP, creatinine/BUN, sodium, potassium, magnesium, bicarbonate, and hearing symptoms.","Warning flags: Contraindicated in anuria. Stop or escalate for severe hypotension, worsening oliguria/azotemia, symptomatic electrolyte depletion, or hearing change. Rapid/high-dose IV exposure increases ototoxicity risk. Per UpToDate: avoid combining with other ototoxins—aminoglycosides, cisplatin, and ethacrynic acid. When response is poor, reassess perfusion, obstruction, dose adequacy, absorption history, and diuretic resistance before simply escalating.\n\nSource: DailyMed furosemide injection label"],
["Magnesium sulfate","Electrolyte / antiarrhythmic","IV magnesium is used for hypomagnesemia replacement and torsades/polymorphic VT with prolonged QT. The urgency, renal function, rhythm, symptoms, and measured level determine the dose and rate.\n\nDosing: Torsades with a pulse: 1–2 g IV diluted over 15 minutes—not faster, since rapid infusion may cause hypotension (UpToDate correction—the old 5–15 minute range is removed); repeat or continue only per ACLS/local protocol. Nonemergent replacement thresholds and rates are protocol-dependent.\n\nDilution/preparation: Common premixes include 1 g/100 mL, 2 g/50 mL, and 4 g/100 mL. Verify grams versus milligrams and whether a concentrated product requires dilution.","Titration & monitoring: Recheck magnesium at the protocol interval; trend potassium, calcium, renal function, urine output, BP, respiratory status, and reflexes. Use ECG monitoring for unstable arrhythmia or rapid administration.","Warning flags: Reduce or avoid repeated empiric dosing in renal impairment. Stop and escalate for hyporeflexia, weakness, respiratory depression, major hypotension/bradycardia, heart block, or worsening conduction. Per UpToDate: heart block is a labeled contraindication, not just a stop sign; use extreme caution in myasthenia gravis and neuromuscular disease. Correcting magnesium can improve refractory hypokalemia, but the two deficits still require separate assessment.\n\nSource: Cooper University adult electrolyte replacement protocol; San Francisco EMS magnesium protocol"],
["Thrombolytics (alteplase / tenecteplase)","Thrombolytic","Thrombolytics (fibrinolytics) dissolve formed clot by converting plasminogen to plasmin; alteplase and tenecteplase are the ICU workhorses. Indications are protocol-driven: acute ischemic stroke, high-risk pulmonary embolism, and STEMI.\\n\\nDosing: Acute ischemic stroke: alteplase 0.9 mg/kg IV (max 90 mg)—10% as bolus over 1 minute, remainder over 60 minutes; tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg IV (max 25 mg) single bolus over 5 seconds followed by a saline flush is an accepted alternative with similar efficacy and safety. High-risk PE (low bleeding risk): alteplase 100 mg IV over ≤2 hours. Follow the active stroke/PE/MI order set—never improvise dosing.\\n\\nDilution/preparation: Reconstitute per label immediately before use; verify weight-based calculations with an independent double-check.","Titration & monitoring: Continuous neuro checks and vitals. BP must be <185/110 before thrombolysis and maintained <180/105 during and for 24 hours after; treat per protocol. Stop the infusion for neurologic decline, new headache, nausea/vomiting, sudden BP rise, or bleeding, and obtain emergent imaging. Watch for orolingual angioedema (1–8%; increased risk with ACE-inhibitor use)—have airway equipment ready. For 24 hours after stroke thrombolysis: no anticoagulants, antiplatelets, or DOACs; avoid intra-arterial, bladder, and NG catheters when safely possible.\\n\\nWarning flags: Absolute contraindications (stroke IVT): intracranial hemorrhage on imaging; intracranial or intraspinal surgery within 14 days; moderate-to-severe TBI within 14 days; spinal cord injury within 3 months; active internal bleeding; severe coagulopathy; platelets <100,000. Screen with the institutional checklist before every dose.","ICU pearl: The biggest bedside failure is delay—confirm last-known-well, weight, BP, glucose, and the contraindication checklist in parallel, not in sequence. Document exact bolus and infusion start times; door-to-needle under 60 minutes is the goal.\\n\\nSource: UpToDate — IV thrombolytic therapy for acute ischemic stroke; reperfusion therapy for high-risk PE (literature current through Aug 2026)"]
];
const dx=[
["Septic shock","Shock","Septic shock is infection-driven circulatory and cellular dysfunction. Vasodilation, endothelial leak, maldistributed flow, and sometimes septic cardiomyopathy reduce effective perfusion even when blood pressure appears acceptable. Assessment cues: Suspected infection; hypotension or rising vasopressor need; altered mentation; cool, mottled, or unusually warm skin; delayed capillary refill; oliguria; elevated or rising lactate; new organ dysfunction. Review likely source, cultures, antimicrobial timing, fluid history, and cardiac function.","Management steps: Activate the sepsis pathway; obtain ordered cultures without delaying antimicrobials; give ordered antibiotics promptly; assess fluid responsiveness rather than relying on static numbers; titrate vasopressors to the ordered MAP goal; trend perfusion, urine output, lactate, capillary refill, oxygenation, glucose, and source-control progress. Reassess after every fluid bolus or vasoactive change for benefit and overload. Per UpToDate: initial 30 mL/kg crystalloid suggested (Grade 2B) with balanced crystalloids preferred over saline (2025 meta-analysis: lower 90-day mortality); lactate >2 mmol/L is the elevated threshold—recheck q6h; MAP targets individualized (recent meta-analyses support lower 60–70 mmHg targets rather than higher); norepinephrine first-line, vasopressin ≤0.03 units/min as add-on; pursue source control within 6–12 h.","Red flags: Escalating pressor dose, rising lactate, worsening mottling, oliguria, altered mentation, new hypoxemia, arrhythmia, or concern for an uncontrolled source. Clinical pearl: A MAP at goal does not prove adequate perfusion; integrate mentation, urine output, skin findings, lactate trend, and cardiac function.\n\nSource: Surviving Sepsis Campaign Guidelines 2021 — SCCM"],
["Cardiogenic shock","Shock","Cardiogenic shock is inadequate forward flow from pump failure, commonly due to acute ischemia, severe ventricular dysfunction, mechanical complication, or dysrhythmia. Elevated filling pressures may produce pulmonary or systemic congestion while low cardiac output causes organ hypoperfusion. Assessment cues: Hypotension or narrow pulse pressure; cool extremities; altered mentation; oliguria; rising lactate; pulmonary edema; jugular venous distention; new murmur; ischemic symptoms; dysrhythmia; or echo evidence of LV, RV, or biventricular failure.","Management steps: Escalate for rapid diagnostic clarification; obtain ECG, labs, and bedside echocardiography as ordered; treat the precipitating cause; maintain oxygenation and perfusion; titrate vasoactive or inotropic support to the ordered hemodynamic goal; track fluid balance closely; and prepare for coronary, structural, or mechanical-circulatory-support evaluation when indicated. Reassess after interventions because the phenotype can evolve or become mixed.","Red flags: Worsening pulmonary edema, recurrent ischemia, malignant dysrhythmia, mechanical complication, increasing lactate, falling urine output, or rising support requirement. Clinical pearl: “Wet” and “cold” can coexist—more fluid may worsen congestion without improving forward flow. Per UpToDate: norepinephrine is the preferred vasopressor (avoid epinephrine when possible); involve a multidisciplinary shock team early; DanGer-Shock (2024) showed early Impella CP reduced 180-day mortality in MI-related cardiogenic shock.\n\nSource: ESICM consensus on circulatory shock and hemodynamic monitoring"],
["Hypovolemic shock","Shock","Hypovolemic shock results from inadequate circulating volume, either hemorrhagic or nonhemorrhagic. Reduced venous return lowers stroke volume and cardiac output; compensatory tachycardia and vasoconstriction may preserve pressure until late. Assessment cues: Bleeding or major fluid loss; tachycardia; narrowed pulse pressure; cool clammy skin; delayed capillary refill; flat neck veins; oliguria; altered mentation; rising lactate; falling hemoglobin; or poor response to position and resuscitation.","Management steps: Identify and control the source; activate hemorrhage or transfusion pathways when indicated; obtain adequate access; administer ordered blood products or fluids; prevent hypothermia; trend hemodynamics, perfusion, urine output, lactate, hemoglobin, coagulation, calcium, and response to each intervention. Look for concealed loss in the chest, abdomen, pelvis, retroperitoneum, or GI tract. Per UpToDate: vasopressors generally should NOT be given—they do not correct the primary problem (only as a bridge in refractory shock); crystalloids are first-line (Grade 1B); hydroxyethyl starch is recommended against (Grade 1B); check lactate and chemistries within 6 hours.","Red flags: Ongoing blood loss, transient response followed by deterioration, worsening acidosis, coagulopathy, hypothermia, hypocalcemia, or increasing vasopressor need before volume/source control. Clinical pearl: A normal blood pressure does not exclude major volume loss; trends and tissue-perfusion findings often change first.\n\nSource: Management of circulatory shock and hypotension"],
["Obstructive shock","Shock","Obstructive shock is low cardiac output caused by a mechanical barrier to filling or ejection, classically massive pulmonary embolism, cardiac tamponade, or tension pneumothorax. The ventricle may be intrinsically capable but cannot fill or empty effectively. Assessment cues: Sudden hypotension; tachycardia; syncope; jugular venous distention; unilateral absent breath sounds; acute hypoxemia; chest pain; pulsus paradoxus; electrical alternans; RV strain; or abrupt deterioration during positive-pressure ventilation.","Management steps: Call for immediate provider/rapid-response evaluation; support oxygenation and perfusion; obtain focused ultrasound (per UpToDate, bedside POCUS—RUSH/FoCUS—to identify tamponade/PE/tension) and other emergent diagnostics without delaying definitive treatment; prepare for cause-specific intervention such as decompression, pericardial drainage, or PE reperfusion pathway; and reassess after each step. Do not delay vasopressors for central access—peripheral pressors are acceptable (per UpToDate). Avoid routine fluid loading when RV failure or tamponade physiology is uncertain—use ordered, response-guided therapy.","Red flags: Peri-arrest physiology, rapidly rising oxygen or pressor need, severe RV dysfunction, loss of pulse, unilateral absent breath sounds, or tamponade signs. Clinical pearl: Pressors can buy time, but obstruction must be relieved; sudden unexplained collapse should trigger a fast “PE–tamponade–tension” check.\n\nSource: Circulatory shock in adults: RUSH assessment"],
["ARDS","Respiratory","ARDS is diffuse inflammatory lung injury that increases alveolar-capillary permeability, producing noncardiogenic edema, reduced compliance, shunt, and impaired oxygenation. Ventilation can worsen injury if tidal volume or pressure overstretches recruitable lung. Assessment cues: Acute hypoxemia after a known insult; bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusions or collapse; escalating FiO2/PEEP; reduced compliance; low P/F ratio; tachypnea; and ventilator dyssynchrony. Consider competing or contributing cardiogenic edema.","Management steps: Use the ordered lung-protective strategy based on predicted—not actual—body weight; per UpToDate numeric targets: initial VT 6 mL/kg PBW (Grade 1B), plateau ≤30 cm H2O, driving pressure <15 cm H2O suggested, SpO2 88–95% (PaO2 55–80 mmHg); prone ventilation is Grade 1B when P/F <150 despite low tidal volume ventilation; HFOV is recommended against (Grade 2B), as is routine ultra-low 4 mL/kg VT. Trend plateau pressure, driving pressure when used locally, P/F ratio, ABGs, SpO2, compliance, and synchrony; minimize unnecessary FiO2 while meeting the ordered target; coordinate sedation, proning, neuromuscular blockade, fluid strategy, and rescue therapies through the unit pathway. Protect tubes, lines, eyes, skin, and pressure points during proning.","Red flags: Refractory hypoxemia, rising plateau pressure, sudden compliance loss, barotrauma, severe acidosis, hemodynamic collapse, or unsafe dyssynchrony. Clinical pearl: Small tidal volumes are tied to predicted body weight because lung size tracks height and sex—not current body weight.\n\nSource: Official ATS update on adult ARDS management"],
["COPD exacerbation","Respiratory","A COPD exacerbation is an acute increase in airway inflammation, bronchospasm, mucus, and expiratory flow limitation. Incomplete exhalation can cause dynamic hyperinflation and intrinsic PEEP, increasing work of breathing and impairing venous return. Assessment cues: Increased dyspnea, cough, or sputum; wheeze; prolonged expiration; accessory-muscle use; diminished air movement; rising PaCO2; acidosis; fatigue; altered mentation; or a “silent” chest. Look for triggers and mimics such as infection, heart failure, PE, pneumothorax, or dysrhythmia.","Management steps: Titrate oxygen to the ordered target (per UpToDate, 88–92% in COPD); administer bronchodilators, steroids (per UpToDate, prednisone 40 mg daily × 5 days), and antibiotics when ordered; IV magnesium sulfate 2 g over 20 min for respiratory failure or severe non-responders (Grade 2C); trend work of breathing, mental status, gas exchange, secretion burden, and treatment response; use NIV as the preferred ventilatory support when appropriate; and, if mechanically ventilated, allow adequate expiratory time and monitor for auto-PEEP. UpToDate correction: mucoactive agents, methylxanthines, and mechanical secretion-clearance techniques have no documented benefit in COPD exacerbations—do not rely on them as therapy. Avoid unnecessary respiratory depressants.","Red flags: Worsening acidosis, decreasing consciousness, exhaustion, refractory hypoxemia, inability to clear secretions, hemodynamic compromise, or NIV failure. Per UpToDate, acute life-threatening features include need for FiO2 ≥40%, PaCO2 >60, or acidosis with pH ≤7.25. Clinical pearl: A falling respiratory rate can signal improvement—or fatigue. Pair it with mental status, tidal volume, gas exchange, and overall effort.\n\nSource: Care of the hospitalized patient with acute COPD exacerbation"],
["Pulmonary edema","Respiratory","Pulmonary edema is fluid accumulation in the interstitium and alveoli from elevated hydrostatic pressure, increased permeability, or both. Cardiogenic edema commonly reflects elevated left-sided filling pressure; noncardiogenic edema requires a different cause-directed plan. Assessment cues: Acute dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, frothy sputum, hypoxemia, diffuse opacities, hypertension or hypotension, jugular venous distention, peripheral edema, changing weight, and increased work of breathing. Use history, echo, imaging, and response to therapy to refine the cause.","Management steps: Sit upright when tolerated; support oxygenation and ventilation; prepare for CPAP/BiPAP when ordered and appropriate; treat the precipitant; administer ordered diuretic or vasodilator therapy—per UpToDate, furosemide 40–100 mg IV initial (diuretic-naïve) or 2–2.5× the home dose; onset 30–120 min, peak 1–2 h; double the dose if no response at 2 h; a spot urine Na <50–70 mEq/L at 2 h suggests insufficient diuretic response (consider sequential nephron blockade); generally continue SGLT2 inhibitors during admission. Track respiratory effort, SpO2/ABG, BP, urine output, net balance, weight, renal function, and electrolytes. Reassess frequently because positive pressure, preload reduction, and diuresis can rapidly change hemodynamics.","Red flags: Escalating oxygen need, pink frothy secretions, severe hypertension, hypotension, ischemia, dysrhythmia, worsening acidosis, exhaustion, or declining mentation. Clinical pearl: “Wet lungs” do not always mean whole-body volume overload; establish the mechanism before assuming more diuresis is the answer.\n\nSource: ACC/AHA/HFSA and ESC heart-failure guideline comparison"],
["Pulmonary embolism","Respiratory","A pulmonary embolism obstructs pulmonary arterial flow, increasing pulmonary vascular resistance and RV afterload. Acute RV dilation can reduce LV filling and cardiac output, causing obstructive shock. Assessment cues: Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, unexplained tachycardia or hypoxemia, syncope, hemoptysis, unilateral leg findings, elevated dead-space ventilation, new RV strain, or hypotension. Review bleeding risk, anticoagulant history, recent surgery or immobility, cancer, and prior VTE.","Management steps: Support oxygenation and perfusion; obtain ordered ECG, biomarkers, CT imaging, and echocardiography; initiate and monitor anticoagulation per protocol; use careful, response-guided fluids if RV preload is inadequate; and activate PERT or the reperfusion pathway for deterioration or high-risk features. Per UpToDate (2026 multisociety PE guideline): high-risk PE with low bleeding risk → alteplase 100 mg over ≤2 h (Grade 1B); high-risk with high bleeding risk → catheter-directed therapy (Grade 2C); intermediate-risk with concerning features (hypotension, normotensive-shock signs, HR >120, syncope, elevated lactate) → catheter-directed therapy suggested (Grade 2C). Track BP, HR, oxygen requirement, rhythm, lactate, RV function, bleeding, platelets, and anticoagulation assays when applicable.","Red flags: Persistent hypotension, syncope, rapidly increasing oxygen need, severe RV dysfunction, rising lactate, altered mentation, or cardiac arrest. Clinical pearl: In acute RV failure, excessive fluid can worsen dilation and septal shift; resuscitation must be cautious and repeatedly reassessed.\n\nSource: 2026 AHA/ACC multisociety guideline for acute pulmonary embolism"],
["Acute kidney injury","Renal","AKI is an abrupt loss of renal filtration and homeostatic function caused by impaired perfusion, intrinsic renal injury, obstruction, or a combination. Creatinine may lag behind injury, so urine output and trend matter. Assessment cues: Rising creatinine from baseline; oliguria; fluid accumulation; hyperkalemia; acidosis; uremic symptoms; new nephrotoxin exposure; hemodynamic instability; pigment injury; or obstruction clues. Review baseline renal function, medication and contrast exposure, fluid balance, bladder drainage, and hemodynamics.","Management steps: Measure urine output accurately; verify catheter patency when present; trend creatinine, BUN, potassium, bicarbonate, magnesium, phosphate, weight, and cumulative balance; optimize perfusion without causing overload; avoid or adjust nephrotoxins and renally cleared drugs with pharmacy/provider guidance; hold ACEi/ARBs/SGLT2i during AKI (per UpToDate); investigate obstruction; and prepare for nephrology or renal-replacement evaluation when indicated. Per UpToDate, urgent KRT indications include K+ >6.5 mEq/L or symptomatic hyperkalemia, pH <7.1 with hypervolemia, diuretic-refractory pulmonary edema, uremic complications, or anuria >24 h. A furosemide stress test (80 mg IV with >200 mL urine over 2 h) suggests an adequate tubular response.","Red flags: Refractory hyperkalemia, severe acidosis, pulmonary edema, uremic complications, rapidly worsening oliguria/anuria, or toxin accumulation. Clinical pearl: Stage AKI from the worst qualifying creatinine or urine-output criterion; a “stable” creatinine can be falsely reassuring during rapidly changing volume or muscle mass.\n\nSource: KDIGO-based AKI detection and management review"],
["DKA / HHS","Endocrine","DKA is insulin deficiency with ketone production and metabolic acidosis; HHS is profound hyperglycemia and hyperosmolality with little or no significant ketoacidosis. Both cause osmotic diuresis, dehydration, and major electrolyte shifts, and overlap is common. Assessment cues: Polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, tachycardia, Kussmaul respirations, abdominal symptoms, ketones, high anion gap, altered mentation, or very high osmolality. Identify the trigger—often infection, missed insulin, ischemia, medication effect, or new diabetes.","Management steps: Follow the institutional fluid, insulin, and electrolyte pathway; verify potassium before and during insulin; trend glucose, beta-hydroxybutyrate or ketones, anion gap, bicarbonate, pH, sodium, potassium, phosphate, creatinine, osmolality, urine output, and neurologic status. Add dextrose when ordered so insulin can continue until ketoacidosis resolves. Per UpToDate: withhold insulin if K+ <3.5 and replete first; add dextrose when glucose <250, keeping 150–200 until resolution; resolution = beta-hydroxybutyrate <0.6 OR anion gap ≤12 plus pH ≥7.3 or bicarbonate ≥18; do NOT use nitroprusside ketones to monitor resolution; overlap IV insulin 1–2 h after starting subcutaneous insulin. Treat the precipitating cause and plan the transition to subcutaneous insulin carefully.","Red flags: Potassium too low for safe insulin, arrhythmia, shock, worsening mental status, cerebral edema concern, rapidly shifting sodium/osmolality, hypoglycemia, or treatment-related fluid overload. Clinical pearl: Glucose often corrects before the metabolic crisis; do not stop therapy based on glucose alone.\n\nSource: Hyperglycemic crises in adults: 2024 consensus report summary"],
["GI bleeding","Hematology","GI bleeding ranges from occult loss to exsanguinating hemorrhage. Hemodynamic impact depends on rate and volume of loss, baseline reserve, and anticoagulant use; hemoglobin may initially appear normal before equilibration. Assessment cues: Hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis, melena, hematochezia, occult blood loss, orthostasis, tachycardia, hypotension, syncope, abdominal symptoms, or rising BUN relative to creatinine. Review liver disease, varices, prior ulcers, NSAIDs, antiplatelets, anticoagulants, and recent procedures.","Management steps: Establish adequate access; send CBC, coagulation testing, type and screen/crossmatch, and ordered chemistries; activate hemorrhage and transfusion pathways when indicated; per UpToDate: use a restrictive transfusion threshold of Hgb 7 g/dL (Grade 2B); pursue endoscopy within 24 h (12–24 h if variceal bleeding is suspected); give high-dose PPI bolus (e.g., esomeprazole 80 mg); NG lavage is not routine or reliable for localizing the source. Administer ordered resuscitation, acid suppression, vasoactive therapy, antibiotics, or reversal agents based on suspected source; track hemodynamics, bleeding appearance and volume, hemoglobin trend, perfusion, urine output, calcium, and coagulopathy; prepare for endoscopic or interventional source control.","Red flags: Ongoing bright-red bleeding, shock, rapid transfusion need, worsening lactate, chest pain/ischemia, severe coagulopathy, airway contamination risk, or suspected variceal hemorrhage. Clinical pearl: Hematochezia with instability can be a brisk upper-GI bleed—do not let stool color alone localize the source.\n\nSource: ACG Clinical Guideline: Upper Gastrointestinal and Ulcer Bleeding"],
["DIC","Hematology","DIC is systemic, dysregulated coagulation triggered by another illness—commonly sepsis, trauma, malignancy, obstetric catastrophe, or major tissue injury. Widespread thrombin generation causes microvascular thrombosis and organ injury while platelets and clotting factors are consumed, producing simultaneous bleeding risk. Assessment cues: Oozing from lines, bruising, mucosal or surgical bleeding, acral ischemia, thrombosis, falling platelets, rising D-dimer/fibrin markers, prolonged PT/aPTT, and falling fibrinogen. Interpret serial trends with the clinical picture.","Management steps: Treat the underlying trigger urgently; trend platelets, PT/INR, aPTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer, hemoglobin, organ perfusion, bleeding, and thrombosis; calculate the institutional/ISTH score when used; minimize unnecessary punctures; and give blood components or anticoagulation only for the specific clinical indication under the active plan.","Red flags: Active major bleeding, intracranial or pulmonary bleeding concern, rapidly falling fibrinogen/platelets, new ischemia, organ failure, or worsening shock. Clinical pearl: DIC is dynamic; a single “not diagnostic” panel does not exclude evolution. Repeat studies when suspicion remains high. Per UpToDate: antifibrinolytics (tranexamic acid, EACA) are generally contraindicated in DIC; a normal-range fibrinogen can mask consumption in sepsis/inflammation because fibrinogen is an acute-phase reactant; AKI occurs in 25–40% of acute DIC.\n\nSource: British Society for Haematology DIC guideline"],
["Status epilepticus","Neuro","Status epilepticus is prolonged or recurrent seizure activity without recovery; convulsive status is treated as an emergency once seizure activity persists beyond the early self-terminating window. Ongoing electrical seizure may continue after visible convulsions stop. Assessment cues: Time of onset; continuous or recurrent motor activity; failure to regain baseline; gaze deviation; subtle twitching; unexplained coma; hypoxia; fever; glucose or electrolyte abnormality; medication nonadherence; intoxication/withdrawal; or acute CNS injury.","Management steps: Record exact seizure and medication times; protect the patient from injury; support airway, breathing, circulation, and oxygenation; check glucose promptly; administer first- and second-line therapy exactly per the active status protocol. Per UpToDate: first-line lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV (max 2 mg/min); IM midazolam 10 mg is superior prehospital because it is delivered faster; second-line agents are equally effective per ESETT—levetiracetam 60 mg/kg (max 4500 mg), fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg, or valproate 40 mg/kg. Refractory status (not ceasing with two appropriately dosed agents) requires midazolam, propofol, or pentobarbital infusion plus intubation/ventilation. Obtain ordered labs and imaging; connect EEG when available if mental status does not recover—nonconvulsive status occurs in about 14%. Monitor respiratory depression, hypotension, rhythm, temperature, and recurrence.","Red flags: Persistent seizure, recurrent activity without awakening, apnea, hypoxemia, hypotension, hyperthermia, severe acidosis, pregnancy, trauma, or continued coma after motor activity stops. Clinical pearl: “Convulsions stopped” is not the same as “status ended”; recovery to baseline or EEG seizure cessation is the endpoint.\n\nSource: Adult status epilepticus acute-management guidance"],
["Increased ICP","Neuro","Intracranial pressure rises when expanding brain tissue, blood, or CSF exhausts compensatory space. As ICP approaches arterial pressure, cerebral perfusion falls and herniation may occur. Assessment cues: Declining consciousness, new agitation, headache/vomiting, pupillary asymmetry or nonreactivity, new weakness/posturing, seizure, worsening CT findings, or Cushing physiology. Interpret the neurologic exam alongside sedation, paralysis, temperature, sodium, ventilation, and hemodynamics.","Management steps: Escalate any acute neurologic change immediately; keep the head elevated and neck midline when not contraindicated; prevent hypoxemia, fever, hypotension, pain, agitation, coughing, and unnecessary stimulation; trend pupils, GCS/neuro exam, MAP, ICP/CPP when monitored, sodium, osmolality, renal function, and fluid balance; administer ordered hyperosmolar therapy and coordinate imaging or surgical evaluation through the neurocritical pathway. Per UpToDate: treat ICP >20 mmHg persisting >5–10 minutes; target CPP (MAP − ICP) >60 mmHg; plateau (A) waves of 50–100 mmHg signal lost compliance and imminent decompensation; hypertonic saline is increasingly used first-line over mannitol (meta-analyses show greater ICP-lowering efficacy)—mannitol 1 g/kg bolus of 20% solution, watching for Na >150 or osmolality >320; glucocorticoids worsen outcomes in TBI-related intracranial hypertension (CRASH)—avoid except for tumor/abscess-related vasogenic edema.","Red flags: New fixed/dilated pupil, rapid GCS decline, posturing, recurrent seizure, Cushing triad, abrupt ICP spike, or loss of protective reflexes. Clinical pearl: Avoid routine prolonged hyperventilation; it can reduce cerebral blood flow. Temporary ventilation changes for impending herniation require immediate provider direction while definitive therapy is mobilized.\n\nSource: Neurocritical Care Society guideline for acute cerebral edema"],
["Post-cardiac arrest syndrome","Cardiac","After ROSC, whole-body ischemia-reperfusion can cause brain injury, myocardial dysfunction, systemic inflammation, vasoplegia, and recurrence of the arrest trigger. Early neurologic findings are often confounded by sedation, paralysis, temperature, and metabolic abnormalities. Assessment cues: Hypotension, dysrhythmia, ischemic ECG changes, ventricular dysfunction, hypoxemia, abnormal PaCO2, seizures/myoclonus, fever, glucose derangement, rising lactate, or evolving multiorgan injury.","Management steps: Follow the institutional post-ROSC pathway; secure oxygenation and ventilation while avoiding unnecessary hyperoxia; target ordered PaCO2 and blood pressure goals; identify and treat the arrest cause; use protocolized temperature control and fever prevention; trend ECG/rhythm, hemodynamics, lactate, glucose, electrolytes, urine output, neurologic exam, and EEG when indicated. Per UpToDate: normocapnia PaCO2 35–45 mmHg; SpO2 94–96%; PaO2 65–100 mmHg (avoid hyperoxia >100 and hypoxemia <65); MAP goal 65–80 mmHg (targeting above 65 may not add benefit) with norepinephrine first-line; temperature ≤37.5°C for 72 hours (fever prevention); delay formal prognostication until ≥72 h after ROSC, after sedative/paralytic clearance and fever treatment. Prevent secondary brain injury and delay formal prognostication until recommended timing and confounders are addressed.","Red flags: Recurrent arrest or dysrhythmia, worsening shock, severe hypoxemia, extreme PaCO2, seizure activity, fever, rapidly rising lactate, or new organ failure. Clinical pearl: The goal is not simply “normal numbers”; avoid hypotension, hypoxemia, hyperoxia, fever, and major ventilation swings while individualizing perfusion to the patient.\n\nSource: 2025 AHA post–cardiac arrest care guideline"],
["Hyperkalemia","Electrolyte emergency","Severe potassium elevation can disrupt cardiac conduction and cause fatal dysrhythmias, even when the initial ECG is normal. Assessment cues: Confirm the result when delay is safe and assess for hemolysis, but do not delay treatment for severe elevation or instability. Place on continuous cardiac monitoring, obtain a 12-lead ECG, repeat potassium, and assess renal function, acid-base status, glucose, medication contributors, tissue injury, and dialysis history.","Management steps: IV calcium stabilizes the myocardium when ECG changes or instability are present but does not lower potassium. Never mix calcium in bicarbonate-containing solutions (calcium carbonate precipitation). Shift potassium intracellularly with insulin/dextrose and nebulized albuterol per order; after the insulin/dextrose bolus, run a D10% infusion at 50–75 mL/hr (a bolus alone is insufficient) and check glucose hourly × 5–6 h—hypoglycemia occurs in 6–19%, and insulin's half-life is prolonged in CKD. Consider bicarbonate when significant metabolic acidosis is present. Remove potassium with appropriate diuresis, binder, or dialysis; per UpToDate, sodium zirconium cyclosilicate (preferred) or patiromer can be used as adjunct therapy in hyperkalemic emergency, while sodium polystyrene sulfonate should not be routine. In digitalis-toxic patients, give calcium gluconate diluted in 100 mL D5W over 20–30 min. Recheck potassium for rebound and monitor glucose closely for delayed hypoglycemia after insulin.","Red flags: ECG changes, potassium above 6.5 mEq/L, rapid rise, weakness or paralysis, acidosis, oligo-anuria, ongoing tissue breakdown, or poor response require urgent escalation. Clinical pearl: A normal ECG does not rule out dangerous hyperkalemia; separate membrane stabilization, intracellular shifting, and potassium removal in your mental checklist. Source: ACEP adult hyperkalemia algorithm"],
["Hyponatremia","Electrolyte emergency","Risk depends on symptom severity, sodium level, and speed of onset. Assessment cues: Look for headache, nausea, confusion, seizures, coma, or respiratory compromise. Determine whether onset is acute, chronic, or unknown. Check glucose and serum osmolality, then evaluate volume status, urine output, urine osmolality, urine sodium, medications, and renal, adrenal, or thyroid causes as ordered.","Management steps: For severe neurologic symptoms, a common US regimen is 3% saline 100 mL IV over 10 minutes, repeated up to twice if symptoms persist (300 mL total); it can be given peripherally—do not delay for a central line (per UpToDate). The active order set controls. Aim initially for an improvement-producing rise of about 4–6 mmol/L, not normalization. Perform frequent sodium and neurologic checks, and watch urine output for sudden water diuresis. If sodium is correcting too quickly, stop the driver and notify the provider immediately. For reversible causes, proactive desmopressin 1–2 mcg IV (dDAVP clamp) at the start of therapy with 3% saline can prevent overcorrection. Note: correcting hypokalemia raises serum sodium mEq-for-mEq and must be counted toward the correction limit.","Red flags: Seizure, coma, worsening confusion, respiratory failure, suspected acute water intoxication, or overcorrection require urgent escalation. Per UpToDate (correction): limit correction to a maximum of 8 mmol/L in any 24-hour period, with a daily goal of only 4–6 mmol/L—target well below the max because actual correction often overshoots the intended rise (the old 8–10 ceiling is removed). Clinical pearl: The safest endpoint is relief of dangerous cerebral-edema symptoms with controlled correction—not a normal sodium on the first day. Source: US/European guideline comparison"],
["Hypertensive emergency","Cardiovascular emergency","Hypertensive emergency is severe blood-pressure elevation with new or worsening acute target-organ injury; the number alone does not define the emergency. Assessment cues: Confirm BP technique and cuff size, repeat the measurement, and assess the brain, arteries, retina, kidneys, and heart. Look for neurologic deficit or encephalopathy, chest or back pain, pulse deficit, pulmonary edema or ischemia, acute kidney injury, pregnancy-related disease, and retinal hemorrhage or papilledema.","Management steps: Place the patient in a closely monitored setting and identify the injured organ before choosing the drug and target. Use a titratable IV antihypertensive appropriate to the syndrome. Unless a diagnosis-specific pathway requires otherwise, lower SBP or MAP by no more than about 20%–25% in the first hour, then toward roughly 160/100 mm Hg over the next 2–6 hours if stable. Reassess neurologic, cardiac, renal, and peripheral perfusion throughout titration.","Red flags: Suspected aortic dissection, acute ischemic stroke, intracranial hemorrhage, ACS or pulmonary edema, eclampsia, or catecholamine crisis requires a syndrome-specific drug, target, and timeline. Per UpToDate additions: in ischemic stroke do not lower BP unless ≥185/110 mmHg in tPA/thrombectomy candidates or ≥220/120 in non-candidates (permissive hypertension otherwise); in sympathomimetic/cocaine-driven emergency, beta-blocker monotherapy is contraindicated (unopposed alpha stimulation)—use phentolamine or nitroprusside; in acute heart failure with pulmonary edema, avoid hydralazine and agents that acutely decrease contractility (e.g., labetalol) in favor of titratable vasodilators. Clinical pearl: Treat the organ injury, not just the monitor value; overly rapid reduction can worsen cerebral, coronary, or renal perfusion. Source: AHA acute-care BP scientific statement"],
["Alcohol withdrawal / CIWA-Ar","Withdrawal","Alcohol withdrawal can progress from tremor and autonomic activation to seizures or delirium tremens. CIWA-Ar measures severity after withdrawal is identified; it is unreliable in intubated, deeply sedated, delirious, or noncommunicative patients. Assessment cues: Establish time of last drink, usual intake, prior withdrawal seizures or DTs, prior sedative needs, and concurrent substances. Trend vital signs, orientation, tremor, diaphoresis, agitation, hallucinations, glucose, electrolytes, and airway or ventilation status while considering alternative causes of delirium.","Management steps: Use the local symptom-triggered, front-loading, or fixed-dose pathway; benzodiazepines are first-line. Phenytoin is ineffective for alcohol withdrawal seizures—benzodiazepines are the treatment (per UpToDate). All patients with withdrawal seizures or DTs require IV benzodiazepine therapy; avoid IM diazepam because absorption is unpredictable. When CIWA-Ar is invalid, use the institution’s ICU pathway, such as a RASS- or MINDS-based approach. Give thiamine as ordered, correct fluid and electrolyte deficits, institute seizure and fall precautions, and reassess respiratory status and cumulative sedative exposure after treatment. Alpha-2 agonists and beta-blockers may mask adrenergic signs but do not treat the withdrawal process.","Red flags: Seizure, delirium tremens, unsafe agitation, severe autonomic instability, hyperthermia, escalating sedative requirement, respiratory depression, or persistent delirium requires escalation and reassessment for another diagnosis. Clinical pearl: Match the assessment scale to the patient’s ability to participate; an invalid CIWA-Ar score can misdirect treatment. Source: ASAM alcohol withdrawal guideline"],
["Delirium / CAM-ICU","Neuro / ICU assessment","Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and cognition; hypoactive delirium is easily missed. Assess arousal before CAM-ICU: RASS -4 or -5 represents coma and is not assessable. Assessment cues: CAM-ICU is positive when acute onset or fluctuation and inattention are present, plus either altered level of consciousness or disorganized thinking. Compare with baseline cognition and look for reversible drivers such as hypoxia or hypercapnia, infection, pain, hypoglycemia, organ dysfunction, withdrawal, medications, urinary retention, constipation, and sleep disruption.","Management steps: Trend RASS and CAM-ICU rather than relying on a single score. Treat the underlying cause and review deliriogenic medications. Consider thiamine supplementation in all patients with delirium—inexpensive and virtually risk-free (per UpToDate). Apply the ABCDEF bundle: pain assessment, SAT/SBT coordination, appropriate analgesia and sedation, delirium monitoring, early mobility, and family engagement. Promote sleep, daytime orientation, hydration, mobility, and access to glasses or hearing aids when appropriate. For severe agitation with safety risk, UpToDate suggests haloperidol 0.5–1 mg as needed, max 5 mg/day, short-term only; avoid haloperidol in parkinsonism (use an atypical agent) and note QTc risk.","Red flags: New focal neurologic deficit, seizure, falling consciousness, severe hypoxemia or hypercapnia, hypoglycemia, sepsis, or unsafe agitation requires prompt evaluation. Clinical pearl: A positive CAM-ICU identifies delirium, not its cause; antipsychotics do not correct the underlying driver and are not routine prevention. Source: SCCM ICU Liberation Bundle"],
["ABG interpreter","Bedside tool","Interpret acid-base data in a fixed sequence: pH, primary process, expected compensation, anion gap and albumin, mixed disorder, then clinical cause. Assessment cues: Review pH, PaCO2, HCO3, chemistry-panel HCO3 when available, sodium, chloride, albumin, and targeted lactate or ketone testing. PaCO2 moves opposite the pH in a primary respiratory disorder; HCO3 moves with the pH in a primary metabolic disorder.","Management steps: For metabolic acidosis, expected PaCO2 = 1.5 × HCO3 + 8 ± 2. For metabolic alkalosis, expected PaCO2 ≈ 0.7 × (HCO3 − 24) + 40 ± 2. Calculate AG = Na − (Cl + HCO3) and compare it with the local laboratory range. When albumin is low, corrected AG ≈ measured AG + 2.5 × (4 − albumin in g/dL). Trend the gas and chemistry with the patient’s ventilation, perfusion, and treatment response.","Red flags: Compensation outside the expected range, near-normal pH with clearly abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3, or a high-gap mismatch suggests a mixed disorder. Clinical pearl: Compensation does not fully normalize or overshoot pH; use the calculation to detect a second process, then connect it to the bedside picture rather than treating the ABG alone. Per UpToDate: compensatory responses do not usually normalize pH—a \"normal\" pH with substantial changes in both HCO3 and PaCO2 signals a mixed disorder, the highest-yield bedside teaching point; venous pH runs 0.03–0.04 units lower than arterial. Source: Merck Manual acid-base disorders"],
["Oxygen-delivery devices","Bedside tool","Delivered FiO2 is estimated unless the device provides a fixed concentration; mask fit, patient minute ventilation, mouth breathing, and equipment all affect delivery. Assessment cues: Identify the ordered SpO2 target and trend oxygen requirement, respiratory rate, work of breathing, mental status, oxygenation, and hypercapnia or acidosis. Match the device to both the oxygen need and the patient’s ventilatory status.","Management steps: Nasal cannula: 1–4 L/min, approximately 25%–40% (per UpToDate—narrowed from the old 1–6 L/min, 24%–44%). Simple mask: 6–10 L/min, approximately 35%–50%; never run below 6 L/min. Nonrebreather: 10–15 L/min, approximately 60%–80%; inflate the reservoir before placement and keep it partially inflated during inspiration. Venturi mask: fixed 24%–60% using the manufacturer-specific adapter and flow. HFNC: up to 60 L/min with independently set flow and FiO2. CPAP supplies one continuous pressure; BiPAP uses separate inspiratory and expiratory pressures. Per UpToDate: uncontrolled oxygen delivery may promote hypercapnia in adults with COPD—an important device-selection caution; actual percent oxygen delivered varies widely with device, mask fit, respiratory rate, tidal volume, and mouth breathing. Reassess after every device or setting change.","Red flags: Rising oxygen requirement, persistent hypoxemia, increasing work of breathing, worsening hypercapnia or acidosis, exhaustion, declining mental status, or lack of improvement requires early airway escalation. NIV may be unsafe with inability to protect the airway, vomiting, copious secretions, severe hemodynamic instability, or an unsuitable facial interface. Clinical pearl: Do not let repeated device changes delay definitive ventilation support when the clinical trajectory is worsening. Source: Open RN oxygenation equipment"],
["CVA / stroke","Neuro","CVA is an abrupt focal neurologic deficit from interrupted cerebral blood flow. Ischemic stroke (about 85% in the US; roughly two-thirds globally) is due to arterial occlusion; hemorrhagic stroke is intracerebral or subarachnoid bleeding. Time of last known well determines reperfusion eligibility. Assessment cues: Sudden unilateral weakness, facial droop, aphasia, neglect, gaze deviation, ataxia, or altered consciousness; NIHSS quantifies severity; non-contrast head CT excludes hemorrhage; CTA of the head and neck identifies large-vessel occlusion for thrombectomy triage.","Management steps: Record exact last-known-well time and activate the stroke pathway immediately. Keep NPO until a swallow screen is passed (aspirin, when indicated, can be given rectally). Trend NIHSS, vitals, glucose, and neuro checks. For ischemic stroke within the window, prepare for IV thrombolysis or thrombectomy per protocol—door-to-needle under 60 minutes (many centers aim for IV thrombolysis start ≤45 min). For hemorrhagic stroke: reverse anticoagulation per protocol, control BP to ordered targets, and obtain emergent neurosurgical evaluation. Avoid routine BP lowering in acute ischemic stroke—permissive hypertension is typical (treat only if >220/120) unless the patient is a thrombolysis/thrombectomy candidate (<185/110 before treatment) or another indication exists. Correct hyperglycemia and fever; start DVT prophylaxis and early mobilization when stable.","Red flags: Declining consciousness, anisocoria, vomiting, severe headache, or new deficit suggest expanding hemorrhage, herniation, or re-occlusion—escalate immediately. Clinical pearl: 'Time is brain'—the sooner thrombolysis starts, the more likely benefit; a missing last-known-well time can forfeit reperfusion options, so pin it down at first contact.\\n\\nSource: UpToDate — initial assessment and management of acute stroke; AHA/ASA acute ischemic stroke guidelines (literature current through Aug 2026)"]
];
const quick=[
['MAP <65 / refractory hypotension','Confirm measurement and rhythm; assess perfusion, volume status, bleeding, sepsis/source control, pump failure, obstruction, and medication effects. Trend lactate/UOP/mentation; escalate early.','Call/escalate for worsening perfusion, rapidly increasing pressor needs, new dysrhythmia, suspected hemorrhage, or concern for obstruction.'],
['Severe metabolic acidosis','Confirm ABG/VBG and chemistry; calculate anion gap; assess lactate, ketones, renal failure, toxins, diarrhea/renal losses. Assess ventilation—bicarbonate can increase CO2 burden.','Escalate severe acidemia, rising lactate, hyperkalemia, declining mental status, or ventilation failure.'],
['Refractory hypoxemia','Check patient first: airway, chest rise, waveform, secretions, tube depth, breath sounds, hemodynamics. Then circuit/ventilator: FiO2 source, disconnection, pressures, PEEP, alarms. Consider edema, ARDS, PE, pneumothorax.','Call immediately for rapidly falling SpO2, unilateral absent sounds, severe distress, instability, or suspected tension physiology.'],
['Post-ROSC','Prioritize airway/ventilation, avoid hypoxemia and hyperoxia, support ordered MAP target, obtain 12-lead/labs, temperature management, and frequent neurologic assessment.','Activate your facility post-arrest pathway; document timing and interventions.'],
['Sudden ventilator deterioration','Use DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment. Disconnect and bag per emergency protocol if indicated; assess patient and call for help.','A sudden pressure, volume, or oxygenation change is an emergency until assessed.']];
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s), app=$('#app');let tab='home',query='',detail=null;let fav=JSON.parse(localStorage.favs||'[]');const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>]/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;'}[c]));
function header(){return `<header class="top"><div class="brand"><div class="mark">+</div><div><div class="eyebrow">Personal bedside brain</div><div class="title">ICU Pocket Reference</div></div></div><button class="back" onclick="show('favorites')">★ ${fav.length}</button></header>`}function list(items,type){const order=type==='med'?['Vasopressor','Vasopressor / inotrope','Inotrope','Antiarrhythmic','Rate control','Antihypertensive infusion','Vasodilator infusion','Sedative','Analgesic','Analgesic / sedative','RSI paralysis','Anticoagulant','Thrombolytic','Loop diuretic infusion','Metabolic','Buffer','Electrolyte / antiarrhythmic']:['Cardiac','Cardiovascular emergency','Respiratory','Shock','Renal','Endocrine','Electrolyte emergency','Hematology','Neuro','Neuro / ICU assessment','Withdrawal','Bedside tool'];let filtered=items.filter(x=>x.join(' ').toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()));const rank=c=>{const i=order.indexOf(c);return i<0?order.length:i};let groups={};filtered.forEach(x=>{(groups[x[1]]=groups[x[1]]||[]).push(x)});let cats=Object.keys(groups).sort((a,b)=>rank(a)-rank(b)||a.localeCompare(b));const btn=x=>`<button class="item" onclick="openDetail('${type}',${items.indexOf(x)})"><h3>${esc(x[0])}</h3>${fav.includes(type+items.indexOf(x))?'<span class="star">★</span>':''}</button>`;return `<h2>${type==='med'?'Medications':'Conditions'}</h2><div class="count">${filtered.length} result${filtered.length===1?'':'s'}</div>${cats.map(c=>`<div class="group">${esc(c)}</div><div class="list">${groups[c].map(btn).join('')}</div>`).join('')||'<div class="empty">No matching reference found.</div>'}`}
function detailView(type,i){let x=(type==='med'?meds:dx)[i],id=type+i,star=fav.includes(id);return `<button class="back" onclick="show('${type==='med'?'meds':'dx'}')">← Back</button><article class="detail"><div class="detail-head"><div><h1>${esc(x[0])}</h1><div class="tag">${esc(x[1])} · general education</div></div><button class="star" onclick="toggleFav('${id}')">${star?'★':'☆'}</button></div><div class="section"><h3>Overview</h3><p>${esc(x[2]).replace(/\n/g,'<br>')}</p></div><div class="section"><h3>Monitor / trend</h3><p>${esc(x[3]).replace(/\n/g,'<br>')}</p></div><div class="section warn"><h3>ICU pearl</h3><p>${esc(x[4]).replace(/\n/g,'<br>')}</p></div>${noteBox(id,x[0])}</article>`}
function noteBox(id,name){return `<div class="protocol"><label>MY WORKPLACE NOTES — PRIVATE / LOCAL ONLY</label><small>Not a hospital policy. Verify against your current orders, policies, and pharmacy guidance.</small><textarea id="note">${esc(localStorage['note-'+id]||'')}</textarea><button class="save" onclick="saveNote('${id}')">Save notes for ${esc(name)}</button></div>`}
function quickView(){return `<h2>Right now</h2><p class="count">General reference only — use emergency response systems and local protocols.</p><div class="list">${quick.map((x,i)=>`<button class="item" onclick="openDetail('quick',${i})"><span class="tag">Problem guide</span><h3>${x[0]}</h3><small>${x[1]}</small></button>`).join('')}</div>`}function quickDetail(i){let x=quick[i];return `<button class="back" onclick="show('quick')">← Back</button><article class="detail"><h1>${x[0]}</h1><div class="section danger"><h3>Rapid bedside check</h3><p>${x[1]}</p></div><div class="section warn"><h3>Communicate / escalate</h3><p>${x[2]}</p></div>${noteBox('quick'+i,x[0])}</article>`}
const calc=[['MAP','(SBP + 2 × DBP) ÷ 3',[['SBP','sbp'],['DBP','dbp']],v=>((v.sbp+2*v.dbp)/3).toFixed(0)+' mmHg'],['Anion gap','Na − (Cl + HCO₃)',[['Na','na'],['Cl','cl'],['HCO₃','hco3']],v=>(v.na-v.cl-v.hco3).toFixed(1)+' mEq/L'],['Corrected sodium','Na + 1.6 × ((glucose − 100) ÷ 100)',[['Na','na'],['Glucose','glu']],v=>(v.na+1.6*((v.glu-100)/100)).toFixed(1)+' mEq/L'],['Corrected calcium','Ca + 0.8 × (4 − albumin)',[['Calcium','ca'],['Albumin','alb']],v=>(v.ca+.8*(4-v.alb)).toFixed(2)+' mg/dL'],['P/F ratio','PaO₂ ÷ FiO₂ (decimal)',[['PaO₂','pao2'],['FiO₂ %','fio2']],v=>(v.pao2/(v.fio2/100)).toFixed(0)],['QTc (Bazett)','QT ÷ √RR (both in seconds)',[['QT ms','qt'],['HR bpm','hr']],v=>{let rr=60/v.hr;return (v.qt/1000/Math.sqrt(rr)*1000).toFixed(0)+' ms'}],['Shock index','HR ÷ SBP',[['HR','hr'],['SBP','sbp']],v=>(v.hr/v.sbp).toFixed(2)],['°C → °F','°F = (°C × 9 ÷ 5) + 32',[['°C','c']],v=>(v.c*9/5+32).toFixed(1)+' °F',true],['kg → lb','lb = kg × 2.205',[['kg','kg']],v=>(v.kg*2.205).toFixed(1)+' lb',true]];
function calcs(){return `<h2>Calculators</h2><p class="count">Check units and interpret with the full clinical picture.</p>${calc.map((c,i)=>`<div class="calc"><h3>${c[0]}</h3><small>${c[1]}</small><div class="fields">${c[2].map(f=>`<label>${f[0]}<input type="number" inputmode="decimal" id="c${i}-${f[1]}" oninput="runCalc(${i})"></label>`).join('')}</div><div class="result" id="r${i}">—</div></div>`).join('')}`}
function notes(){return `<h2>My protocols & notes</h2><div class="protocol"><label>PRIVATE WORKPLACE REFERENCE</label><p>Keep hospital-specific policies, phone numbers, dilution notes, and workflow reminders here. This stays in this browser on this device.</p><textarea id="master-note" placeholder="Example: Sepsis pathway location, rapid response extension, unit-specific titration reminder…">${esc(localStorage.masterNotes||'')}</textarea><button class="save" onclick="localStorage.masterNotes=$('#master-note').value;alert('Saved on this device.')">Save my notes</button></div><div class="section warn"><h3>Safety boundary</h3><p>This app is a personal educational reference, not a prescribing tool or replacement for clinical judgment, orders, current institutional policy, pharmacy guidance, or emergency response procedures.</p></div>`}
function searchResults(){let all=[...meds.map((x,i)=>['med',i,x]),...dx.map((x,i)=>['dx',i,x]),...quick.map((x,i)=>['quick',i,x])].filter(x=>x[2].join(' ').toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()));return `<h2>Search</h2><div class="list">${all.map(x=>`<button class="item" onclick="openDetail('${x[0]}',${x[1]})"><span class="tag">${x[0]==='med'?'Medication':x[0]==='dx'?'Condition':'Problem guide'}</span><h3>${x[2][0]}</h3><small>${x[2][2]}</small></button>`).join('')||'<div class="empty">No matches.</div>'}</div>`}function favorites(){let all=[...meds.map((x,i)=>['med',i,x]),...dx.map((x,i)=>['dx',i,x])].filter(x=>fav.includes(x[0]+x[1]));return `<h2>Favorites</h2><div class="list">${all.map(x=>`<button class="item" onclick="openDetail('${x[0]}',${x[1]})"><span class="tag">${x[2][1]}</span><h3>${x[2][0]}</h3></button>`).join('')||'<div class="empty">Tap ☆ on any medication or condition to keep it here.</div>'}</div>`}function show(x){tab=x;detail=null;query='';render();scrollTo(0,0)}function openDetail(type,i){detail={type,i};render();scrollTo(0,0)}function toggleFav(id){fav=fav.includes(id)?fav.filter(x=>x!==id):[...fav,id];localStorage.favs=JSON.stringify(fav);render()}function saveNote(id){localStorage['note-'+id]=$('#note').value;alert('Saved on this device.')}function runCalc(i){let c=calc[i],v={};c[2].forEach(f=>v[f[1]]=Number($('#c'+i+'-'+f[1]).value));$('#r'+i).textContent=c[2].every(f=>Number.isFinite(v[f[1]])&&(c[4]||v[f[1]]!==0))?c[3](v):'—'}function searchAll(){render()}if('serviceWorker'in navigator)navigator.serviceWorker.register('./sw.js');
const refs=[
['Sepsis & septic shock','Surviving Sepsis Campaign, 2021','https://www.idsociety.org/practice-guideline/surviving-sepsis-campaign-adult-guidelines-2021','Primary guideline for septic-shock content and vasopressor framing.'],
['Cardiac arrest / post-ROSC','American Heart Association: Adult Advanced Life Support','https://cpr.heart.org/en/resuscitation-science/cpr-and-ecc-guidelines/adult-advanced-life-support/','Current AHA guideline hub for adult cardiac arrest and cardiovascular emergencies.'],
['Sedation, analgesia & delirium','SCCM PADIS Guidelines','https://www.sccm.org/clinical-resources/guidelines/guidelines/guidelines-for-the-prevention-and-management-of-pa','Source for general ICU sedation and analgesia concepts.'],
['Acute kidney injury','KDIGO AKI / AKD Guideline hub','https://kdigo.org/guidelines/acute-kidney-injury/','Check current publication status and your local pathway.'],
['Medication details','FDA Drugs@FDA','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cder/daf/','Use current labeling, pharmacy guidance, and local policy for doses and administration.']];
function references(){return `<h2>References</h2><p class="count">Starter sources for this educational build. Check the current guideline and follow local policy where it differs.</p><div class="list">${refs.map(r=>`<a class="item" style="color:var(--text);text-decoration:none" href="${r[2]}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="tag">${r[0]}</span><h3>${r[1]} ↗</h3><small>${r[3]}</small></a>`).join('')}</div><div class="section warn"><h3>Source scope</h3><p>These sources support the general topics; bedside summaries are not line-by-line evidence mapped. Medication doses, dilutions, compatibility, and titration are intentionally omitted: use your pharmacy and institutional references.</p></div>`}
cards=()=>`<div class="grid"><button class="card" onclick="show('meds')"><div class="icon">M</div><strong>Medications</strong><small>${meds.length} quick cards</small></button><button class="card" onclick="show('dx')"><div class="icon">+</div><strong>Conditions</strong><small>${dx.length} ICU essentials</small></button><button class="card" onclick="show('quick')"><div class="icon">!</div><strong>Right now</strong><small>Problem guides</small></button><button class="card" onclick="show('calcs')"><div class="icon">#</div><strong>Calculators</strong><small>7 bedside tools</small></button><button class="card" onclick="show('notes')"><div class="icon">N</div><strong>My protocols</strong><small>Private local notes</small></button><button class="card" onclick="show('refs')"><div class="icon">R</div><strong>References</strong><small>Guidelines & sources</small></button></div>`;
render=function(){let body=detail?(detail.type==='quick'?quickDetail(detail.i):detailView(detail.type,detail.i)):tab==='home'?`<div class="disclaimer"><strong>General education only.</strong> Use current orders, institution policies, pharmacy guidance, and clinical judgment.</div><div class="search">Search <input autofocus placeholder="Search meds, conditions, problems..." value="${esc(query)}" oninput="query=this.value;searchAll()"></div>${query?searchResults():`<h2>Quick access</h2><div class="quick">${quick.slice(0,3).map((x,i)=>`<button class="card" onclick="openDetail('quick',${i})"><div class="tag">Right now</div><strong>${x[0]}</strong></button>`).join('')}</div><h2>Browse</h2>${cards()}`}`:tab==='meds'?list(meds,'med'):tab==='dx'?list(dx,'dx'):tab==='quick'?quickView():tab==='calcs'?calcs():tab==='notes'?notes():tab==='refs'?references():favorites();app.innerHTML=header()+body+`<nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner">${[['home','Home'],['meds','Meds'],['dx','Conditions'],['calcs','Tools'],['notes','Notes']].map(n=>`<button class="${tab===n[0]&&!detail?'active':''}" onclick="show('${n[0]}')">${n[1]}</button>`).join('')}</div></nav>`};render();
